### Page 18 of 160

```text
13. The review and recommendations ofother reviewing bodies.
Compiles
This criterion is addressed because comments raised by City agency reviewers and
the Wyoming Department of Transportation (WYDOT) reviewer have been addressed by the PUD
regulations document and map exhibits.
14. Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general
welfare.,
Complies.
The proposed PUD amendment’s standards and guidelines are expected to guide
development in a way that meets infrastructure needs as well as public service needs to
Sweetgrass residents, workers and visitors. The adopted Sweetgrass Annexation Agreement with
the City identifies provisions for funding these improvements for internal development as well as
improvements to mitigate any off-site impacts to the surrounding area associated with the overall
Sweetgrass development.
15. The application is compatible with the character ofthe surrounding area in
terms ofbuilding scale, building form, landscape, andsite design.
Complies.
As noted above in review criterion Number 8, the PUD development would
introduce a new and different type of development in an area surrounded by rural residential,
higher density mobile home parks, a community college campus, business service and light
industrial businesses, numerous mini-storage facilities, and a cattle ranch. The PUD development
would resemble a master planned community or a suburban community in building scale, building
form, landscape and site design, unlike the rural agriculture and rural residential character of the
existing neighborhood.
However, one can state that the Laramie County Community College is a catalyst for the type of
mixed-use development recommended by PlanCheyenne and proposed in the Sweetgrass PUD.
The proposed PUD amendments would allow a 15-feet increase in building heights within the
Village Center, from 40-feet to 55-feet, and would allow a 20-feet increase in building height in
the area proposed for an event center. However, in comparison with buildings within the LCCC
campus, staff opines that this additional height would be marginally discernible within its
immediate streetscape.
UDC-19-00392 Sweetgrass PUD 1st Amendment
